module

public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Adjoin.FG
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.FiniteType

/-!
# Finite type subalgebras are closed under joins

This file records the fibrewise content of the directedness half of Stacks Tag 05JT: two
subalgebras of finite type over a base ring are both contained in a subalgebra of finite type,
namely the one they jointly generate.  Over an affine open of a scheme, this is exactly the
upper bound that directedness of the finite type quasi-coherent subalgebras of an algebra sheaf
asks for.

## Main results

* `Stacks.RingTheory.FiniteTypeSubalgebra.finiteType_sup`: the join of two subalgebras of finite
  type is of finite type.
* `Stacks.RingTheory.FiniteTypeSubalgebra.fg_range` and
  `Stacks.RingTheory.FiniteTypeSubalgebra.finiteType_sup_range`: the subalgebra generated by the
  images of two algebra maps whose sources are of finite type is of finite type.  This is the
  shape the sheaf-level argument needs, where the two subalgebras of the sections of the ambient
  algebra arise as the images of the inclusions of two subobjects.
* `Stacks.RingTheory.FiniteTypeSubalgebra.finiteType_of_sup_range_eq_top`: if the images of two
  finite type algebras generate the whole algebra, that algebra is of finite type.
* `Stacks.RingTheory.FiniteTypeSubalgebra.finiteType_adjoin_singleton`: a single element
  generates a subalgebra of finite type.  This is the fibrewise content of the colimit half of
  Tag 05JT, where every section has to be exhibited inside some finite type subalgebra.

## Implementation notes

Everything here is assembled from `Subalgebra.FG.sup`, `Subalgebra.FG.map` and
`Subalgebra.fg_iff_finiteType`; the file exists because the consumers want the statements in
`Algebra.FiniteType` form rather than in `Subalgebra.FG` form, and because the passage from an
algebra map to its range needs `Algebra.map_top`.

## What is not done

Nothing here says anything about sheaves.  In particular the passage from these statements to
the corresponding statement about an algebra sheaf requires knowing that the sections of the
subalgebra sheaf generated by two subalgebra sheaves over an affine open are the subalgebra
generated by their sections, which is not proved anywhere in this directory.

## References

* [The Stacks Project, Tag 05JT](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05JT)
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace Stacks.RingTheory.FiniteTypeSubalgebra

variable {R A B C : Type*} [CommRing R] [CommRing A] [CommRing B] [CommRing C]
  [Algebra R A] [Algebra R B] [Algebra R C]

/-- The join of two subalgebras of finite type is of finite type: a finite generating set of the
join is the union of finite generating sets of the two subalgebras. -/
theorem finiteType_sup (S T : Subalgebra R A) (hS : Algebra.FiniteType R S)
    (hT : Algebra.FiniteType R T) : Algebra.FiniteType R (S ⊔ T : Subalgebra R A) :=
  (Subalgebra.fg_iff_finiteType _).1
    (((Subalgebra.fg_iff_finiteType S).2 hS).sup ((Subalgebra.fg_iff_finiteType T).2 hT))

/-- The range of an algebra map whose source is of finite type is a finitely generated
subalgebra. -/
theorem fg_range (b : B →ₐ[R] A) (hB : Algebra.FiniteType R B) : b.range.FG := by
  rw [← Algebra.map_top]
  exact hB.out.map b

/-- The range of an algebra map whose source is of finite type is a subalgebra of finite
type. -/
theorem finiteType_range (b : B →ₐ[R] A) (hB : Algebra.FiniteType R B) :
    Algebra.FiniteType R b.range :=
  (Subalgebra.fg_iff_finiteType _).1 (fg_range b hB)

/-- **The fibrewise upper bound of Stacks Tag 05JT, directedness half.**  The subalgebra
generated by the images of two algebra maps whose sources are of finite type is of finite
type, and it contains both images. -/
theorem finiteType_sup_range (b : B →ₐ[R] A) (c : C →ₐ[R] A) (hB : Algebra.FiniteType R B)
    (hC : Algebra.FiniteType R C) :
    Algebra.FiniteType R (b.range ⊔ c.range : Subalgebra R A) :=
  (Subalgebra.fg_iff_finiteType _).1 ((fg_range b hB).sup (fg_range c hC))

/-- **The affine-local step of Stacks Tag 05JT, directedness half.**  If the images of two
algebras of finite type generate the whole algebra, that algebra is itself of finite type.  This
is the form the sheaf-level argument uses it in: over an affine open the join of two subalgebra
sheaves has to be recognized as the subalgebra generated by their sections. -/
theorem finiteType_of_sup_range_eq_top (b : B →ₐ[R] A) (c : C →ₐ[R] A)
    (hB : Algebra.FiniteType R B) (hC : Algebra.FiniteType R C)
    (h : b.range ⊔ c.range = ⊤) : Algebra.FiniteType R A :=
  ⟨h ▸ (fg_range b hB).sup (fg_range c hC)⟩

/-- A single element generates a subalgebra of finite type. -/
theorem finiteType_adjoin_singleton (a : A) :
    Algebra.FiniteType R (Algebra.adjoin R {a}) :=
  Algebra.FiniteType.adjoin_of_finite (Set.finite_singleton a)

end Stacks.RingTheory.FiniteTypeSubalgebra
